/* The handoff's option row, in its two states, verbatim.
 *
 * THE CLASS NAME IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT IS NOT A FREE CHOICE. Motion.uss
 * carries a `.option-row` rule - transition-property: border-color,
 * background-color, on --motion-quick - added one task before this component
 * existed, and its own header lists `option-row` among four selectors that
 * "match nothing today". Naming this root class anything else returns that
 * rule to matching nothing, silently, and the only symptom is that the row
 * snaps between its two treatments instead of easing into them. Pinned by
 * AnOptionRowsRootClassIsTheOneMotionUssKeysItsTransitionOn.
 *
 * TWO NEAR-IDENTICAL COPIES OF THIS TREATMENT NOW EXIST, and the other one
 * is Theme.uss's `.option` / `.option--selected`. That is deliberate rather
 * than an oversight, and the alternative was measured against and rejected:
 * borrowing Theme's pair would mean this component carrying FOUR classes
 * (option, option-row, option--selected, option-row--selected) with its
 * state expressed in two of them at once, so the first edit that toggled one
 * and not the other would leave the tested state and the rendered state
 * disagreeing with nothing to catch it. Theme's `.option` stays as the
 * vocabulary a screen reaches for when it wants the treatment without the
 * component; this file is what the component itself renders. Both quote the
 * same handoff rule, so if that rule ever changes, both change.
 *
 * NO TYPE RULES HERE. The title and detail labels carry `t-card-title` and
 * `t-secondary` in the UXML, and those already resolve to exactly the
 * font-size and colour a per-component copy would restate - 13px/--ink and
 * 11px/--mute. Theme.uss's header is explicit that re-implementing one of
 * its primitives makes a second source; the type scale is one of them.
 */
.option-row {
    flex-direction: column;
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-width: 1px;
    border-color: var(--hairline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding: var(--space-3);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

/* USS has no inset box-shadow, so the handoff's 2px ring is a real border
 * and the unselected hairline is the same border at 1px - both states carry
 * one, so the layout cost is the same and only the weight and colour move.
 * Theme.uss's `.option` comment records the same reasoning. */
.option-row--selected {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-width: 2px;
    border-color: var(--ring);
}

/* Layout, not type: the gap between the two lines. */
.option-row__detail {
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
}

/* THE PRESS, BECAUSE AN OPTION ROW IS NOT A BUTTON AND INHERITS NOTHING.
 * `.campaign-row:active` used to carry this and went out with the bespoke
 * rule it lived in. A row is a bare element with a ClickEvent handler, so
 * Theme.uss's `Button:active` never applied to it, and Motion.uss's
 * `.option-row` transition only runs on a class change - which a tap is not.
 * The result was a row that did nothing at all under a thumb: no movement, no
 * tint, nothing between the tap and the screen transition. That is the
 * failure mode ScreenScaffold.OnBack's own comment argues against, and it is
 * invisible to both the suite and the corpus, neither of which can render an
 * :active state. Placed on the component rather than one screen, so every
 * consumer gets it. The 1px translate is the handoff's lighter press - the
 * 2px drop and its shadow edge belong to a CTA, not to a list row. */
.option-row:active {
    translate: 0 1px;
    background-color: var(--violet-tint);
}
